WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.240
One channel for instructions and data.

2
00:00:02.520 --> 00:00:11.288
Every attack in this lesson comes from one fact: a language model receives your instructions and the data it works on as one sequence of tokens.

3
00:00:11.568 --> 00:00:17.563
There is no reliable, built-in boundary between “this is what you should do” and “this is what you should read.

4
00:00:17.843 --> 00:00:26.440
” Web developers solved the equivalent problem for databases with parameterized queries, which keep code and data in separate channels.

5
00:00:26.720 --> 00:00:41.269
Nothing comparable exists for natural language, which is why Simon Willison, who coined the term “prompt injection” in 2022, has written about it for years as an unsolved problem (his series is linked in the sources).

6
00:00:41.549 --> 00:00:48.781
Direct prompt injection is the familiar case: the person typing into the system tries to override its instructions.

7
00:00:49.061 --> 00:01:05.467
Indirect prompt injection, named in Greshake and colleagues’ 2023 paper “Not what you’ve signed up for,” is the one that matters for agents: an attacker plants instructions in content the system is likely to retrieve, and never talks to your system at all.

8
00:01:05.747 --> 00:01:14.685
The paper demonstrated it against real products, and catalogued the consequences: data theft, spreading to other users, and manipulated information.

9
00:01:14.965 --> 00:01:27.296
Google’s security team describes the same split, with indirect injections hidden in emails, documents, or calendar invites that instruct the AI to exfiltrate data or take rogue actions.

10
00:01:27.576 --> 00:01:31.288
For the agent you’ll build in this studio, list the channels concretely.

11
00:01:31.568 --> 00:01:44.752
Issue titles and bodies (anyone who can file an issue), comments, linked web pages, files in a repository, tool results from any MCP server, and even the names of labels or users.

12
00:01:45.032 --> 00:01:51.283
Each one is text an outsider may control, and each one lands in the same context as your system prompt.
